Stanislav Traykov <stanislavNoeZpam@tortoises.org> deserves a cookie for saying:
> S. wrote on 09/24/2003 10:09 AM:
>> Yep. Any IDs the character loses, stay lost until they're re-identified 
>> within the context of the game. 

> It's strange enough from a logical point of view that in a given game
> a zinc wand has always the same functionality. Anyway, the (magical)
> process of identifying it just reveals the mapping of zinc to this
> functionality.

True, true.

> Whatever the perception of this association might be like, it is more
> real to the character and she or he is _expected_ to remember it. This
> is also because they have richer (visual, haptic, aural) perception and
> are dealing with such important things as life and death, the avoidance
> of the latter being sometimes courtesy of wands. The player however, may
> forget these associations, and is thus assisted by the interface which
> kindly replaces zinc with a more meaningful name, once the mapping has
> been discovered.

Again, true.  The character is expected to remember certain things.
Unless, of course, his or her brain is devoured by skull-burrowing
tentacles.  Hypothetically.

> How the mind flayers' psychic energy reaches this interface and/or
> supposedly the player's mind is a bit of a mystery. Perhaps, as no
> real forgetting is possible, this is a "best effort".

Definitely.  Everything in the game is, to some extent, a best effort.
Any effects on the interface are simply representative of changes
in-game.  If the interface is assisting you with certain associations
that the character remembers on his or her own, all help from the
interface should vanish when a character forgets those associations
in-game.  Meaning, IMO, that all #namings on any forgotten objects
should be wiped as well.  In the current situation, where #namings
remain, I consider any #namings designed to circumvent amnesia or mind
flayer attacks to be in poor taste at best and cheating at worst.   

> In any case, if I cannot be accused because I happen to remember what
> ELBIB YLOH, HACKEM MUCHE, etc. stand for, why can I be accused if I
> wrote it down?

Once it's IDed, the game "writes it down" for you by changing the name
in the interface.  Writing it down out of game as well serves no purpose
except in preparation for amnesia.  

> Esp., would you recommend players, in order to avoid cheating, to hit
> `\' and memorize the entire list, when they see a mind flayer coming?

No.  

> Also, would it be cheating if I turned off the "assisting features" of
> the interface and let it use the descriptive names (ELBIB YLOH, pink
> potion, ...) for everything (with the `\' command left intact)? Then,
> I would, just as the real character, learn to distinguish objects by
> their use and would have little problems remembering what my stuff is
> (the game will be a bit[?] more tedious however).

It certainly removes the best-effort mechanism for amnesia, effectively
making amnesia a non-issue, but I think the downside of having to
remember everything without interface help is more than a balance.  

